We are making yet another fresh attempt at App::Yath2/Test2::Harness2

Test2::Harness (App::Yath) is a released and well used Test harness.

The way the current 1.0 implementation works is this:
A runner executes all the tests, they write their output to files in a
directory structure. A single process then reads all these output files, turnes
their lines into events, then audits them and injects additional events or
modifies some events in place. The event stream is then fed to loggers and/or
renderers. Everything along the way sees every event from every process.

Problems with the main implementation:
 * Many test processes, but only 1 process reading the events, major bottleneck
 * All events sent everywhere, even when something only cares about little bits.
 * The YathDB database layer stores every event as its own row, which is not necessary and causes major db issues.
 * The current YathUI layer is slow, and clunky
 * Lots of disk IO, and too many handles, polling on files, etc.
 * Uses an older version of what became Getopt::Yath

----

Moving foward

The new 2.0 yath, which will be App::Yath2 and Test2::Harness2 will be a fresh
rewrite that fixes most of these things.

 * Command line arguments will be processed with Getopt::Yath
 * Each test will be run using a collector (Test2-Collector, ~/projects/Test2-Collector)
  * test -> parser -> processors (auditor) -> recorder
  * All events get written to a compressed .jsonl.zst file
  * Transitions get sent to the main harness
 * The main harness will largely be an orchestrator of collectors.
  * Note that collectors may not be direct children, so we need to reply on the transition messages to tell us when one is completed, not reaping
 * Transition events (as well as any added by the harness itself) will be the primary state shared and forwarded to things like renderers.
 * Renderers can look at the .jsonl.zst files as needed based on transitions (the state carried should include the events.jsonl.zst paths for each collector)
 * Every process started by the harness, including the main harness process should be a collector (non-test variant) so that we have a consistency of wire format for all processes, test and otherwise.

